<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sima Qian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 145–c. 86 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sima Qian (c. 145–c. 86 BCE) was a historian of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> who completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a> (Shiji), a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from the legendary sage kings to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>. Castrated as punishment after defending a disgraced general, he chose to live on to finish the work. Its arrangement of annals, treatises and biographies became the model for China's official dynastic histories.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sima-qian.jpg" alt="Sima Qian" width="320" height="327" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Si_maqian.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to traditional histories compiled more than a millennium later, above all the Shiji of <b>Sima Qian</b>, the Shang dynasty was founded when Tang of Shang, a lord of the eastern plain, overthrew Jie, the tyrannical last ruler of the Xia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its coastline supplied salt and fish, its plains grew millet and wheat, and the region was famed for silk and fine textiles; <b>Sima Qian</b> wrote that Qi supplied caps, sashes, clothes and shoes to the whole world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Shiji of <b>Sima Qian</b>, the Ying clan descended from Feizi, a skilled horse breeder whom King Xiao of Zhou rewarded with a small fief at Qin, in the upper Wei and Xihan river valleys of eastern Gansu; tradition dates the grant to about 897 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Chanyu (r. 209–174 BCE), who seized power from his father Touman in a coup described by the historian <b>Sima Qian</b>: Modu trained his followers to shoot their whistling arrows wherever he aimed, executing those who hesitated when he shot at his own favourite horse and then his favourite…</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Shiji of <b>Sima Qian</b>, which draws on the report of the envoy Zhang Qian, they originally lived between Dunhuang and the Qilian Mountains in the Gansu corridor, and in the late third century BCE they were strong enough to look down on the Xiongnu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Already the historian <b>Sima Qian</b>, writing around 100 BCE, was uncertain who Laozi had been, and modern scholars regard the Daodejing as a compilation of sayings that took shape over the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE.</blockquote><a href="/religion/daoism">Daoism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
